-- PINNED REPRO — found by this corpus on 2026-08-10, FIXED 2026-08-10.
--
-- STATUS: live PASS. It was BLOCKED (see gen::blocked_reason) while red; the
-- block is gone because the defect is gone, not because the case was retired.
-- It now asserts the fix and fails if the collision ever comes back.
--
-- HOW IT WAS FIXED (rust/crates/lower)
--   1. `select_record_candidate` (goty.rs) no longer GUESSES. A nominal whose
--      declared field types contradict the record is refuted outright, and when
--      several candidates are equally indistinguishable it resolves to NONE
--      rather than taking `candidates.first()` by registration order.
--   2. A record literal flowing into a nominal record slot adopts that slot
--      (lower.rs, the same expected-type threading `if`/`case`/`let` already
--      use). `mk` now emits `Main_Kv_R{Key: v_0, Value: v_1}` directly — no
--      coercion at all — instead of building an anonymous struct and narrowing.
--
-- WHAT USED TO HAPPEN
--   `sky build` reported "Types OK (1 module(s))" and "Sky lowering succeeded".
--   The program then panicked at runtime:
--
--     Sky panic: CoerceFailure
--     panicMsg=rt.Coerce: expected rt.SkyADT, got string (42)
--     main.Main_mk(...) at sky-out/main.go
--
-- WHY
--   `record_fieldsets` (rust/crates/lower/src/lower.rs:246-266) is keyed on the
--   SORTED FIELD-NAME VECTOR, and its own comment at :256-258 records that
--   "Two records with identical field names but different field types collide
--   here." The stdlib contains
--
--     Std.Analytics.EventProp = { key : String, value : PropValue }
--         (sky-stdlib/Std/Analytics.sky:85-88)
--
--   so a USER record `{ key : String, value : String }` lands on the same
--   `[key, value]` key. `select_record_candidate` is supposed to disambiguate by
--   TYPE, and at the CONSTRUCTION SITE inside an annotated function it picks the
--   stdlib candidate: the `value` parameter is coerced to `PropValue`, an ADT.
--
-- THE PART THAT MAKES IT SEVERE
--   * No import of `Std.Analytics` is required. The stdlib is always in the
--     compilation, so ANY app defining a `{ key, value }` record with a
--     constructor function is affected.
--   * No erasure is required. The previously-recorded form of this bug
--     (2026-08-03) was characterised as needing `fst`/`snd`/tuple destructure to
--     erase the field types. It does not: a plain annotated constructor is
--     enough. This repro is the minimal form.
--   * `{ key, value }` is an extremely common record shape.
--   * It violates two non-negotiable rules at once: "if it compiles, it works"
--     and "no runtime panic from well-typed Sky code".
--
-- THE NEUTRALISED TWIN
--   Renaming the fields to `gkey`/`gvalue` — colliding with nothing in the
--   stdlib — makes the identical program correct. That pairing is what proves
--   the collision is the cause; it is the `collider = local` case of the
--   `fieldset_ctor` stratum.
--
-- expect: accept, stdout "42"
--
-- CHECKED IN, hand-written. Not generator output.
module Main exposing (main)

import Sky.Core.Prelude exposing (..)
import Std.Log exposing (println)


type alias Kv =
    { key : String, value : String }


mk : String -> String -> Kv
mk k v =
    { key = k, value = v }


main =
    println (mk "a" "42").value
